    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "title": "Read and Print One-Word Names in Reverse Order",
    "description": "Write a C program that reads an integer n, then reads n names and prints them in reverse order, one name per line.\n\nRules:\n- 1 <= n <= 5\n- Each name is a one-word string with at most 30 characters.\n- Because the input uses one-word names, names do not contain spaces.\n\nInput:\n- The first line contains n.\n- The next n inputs are names.\n\nOutput:\n- Print the names from last to first, each on its own line.",
    "source_chunk_ids": ["6a7f57855975329719be789d", "6a7f57855975329719be78a0", "6a7f57855975329719be78a8"],
    "skeleton_code": [
      {
        "filename": "main.c",
        "content": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    char names[5][31];\n\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n\n    return 0;\n}\n",
        "is_readonly": false
      }
    ],
    "solution_code": [
      {
        "filename": "main.c",
        "content": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    int i;\n    char names[5][31];\n\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n\n    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        scanf(\"%30s\", names[i]);\n    }\n\n    for (i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        printf(\"%s\\n\", names[i]);\n    }\n\n    return 0;\n}\n"
      }
    ],
