    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "title": "Process Work Progress with Overridden Task Rules",
    "description": "Complete the class hierarchy so that different task types handle the same work action in different ways through method overriding.\n\nRequirements:\n- Task stores a task name and current progress.\n- The method work(int units) is defined in Task and overridden in subclasses.\n- Progress is always kept in the range from 0 to 100.\n- QuickTask behavior:\n  - If units is positive, increase progress by units.\n  - If progress becomes greater than 100, set it to 100.\n  - Non-positive units do nothing.\n- ReviewTask behavior:\n  - If units is not positive, do nothing.\n  - While progress is below 80, work can increase progress, but it cannot go above 80 in that stage.\n  - Once progress is exactly 80, the task needs a final review step.\n  - A later call to work(units) completes the task only when units is at least 20, and then progress becomes 100.\n  - If progress is 80 and units is less than 20, progress stays 80.\n- The main method reads two tasks. For each task, it reads:\n  type name initialProgress firstWork secondWork\n- type is either QUICK or REVIEW.\n- Store both objects in a Task array, call work(firstWork) and then work(secondWork) for each object through the parent reference.\n- Print each result on its own line in the format:\n  name: progress",
    "source_chunk_ids": ["6a7f640b682895cb6c9aff68", "6a7f640b682895cb6c9aff6c"],
    "skeleton_code": [
      {
        "filename": "Main.java",
        "content": "import java.util.*;\n\nclass Task {\n    protected String name;\n    protected int progress;\n\n    public Task(String name, int progress) {\n        this.name = name;\n        if (progress < 0) {\n            this.progress = 0;\n        } else if (progress > 100) {\n            this.progress = 100;\n        } else {\n            this.progress = progress;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void work(int units) {\n    }\n\n    public String getName() {\n        return name;\n    }\n\n    public int getProgress() {\n        return progress;\n    }\n}\n\nclass QuickTask extends Task {\n    public QuickTask(String name, int progress) {\n        super(name, progress);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void work(int units) {\n    }\n}\n\nclass ReviewTask extends Task {\n    public ReviewTask(String name, int progress) {\n        super(name, progress);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void work(int units) {\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        Task[] tasks = new Task[2];\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {\n            String type = sc.next();\n            String name = sc.next();\n            int initialProgress = sc.nextInt();\n            int firstWork = sc.nextInt();\n            int secondWork = sc.nextInt();\n\n            if (type.equals(\"QUICK\")) {\n                tasks[i] = new QuickTask(name, initialProgress);\n            } else {\n                tasks[i] = new ReviewTask(name, initialProgress);\n            }\n\n            tasks[i].work(firstWork);\n            tasks[i].work(secondWork);\n        }\n\n        for (Task task : tasks) {\n            System.out.println(task.getName() + \": \" + task.getProgress());\n        }\n    }\n}\n",
        "is_readonly": false
      }
