    "title": "Simulate Open Addressing with Linear Probing",
    "description": "Write a Java program to simulate a hash table that uses open addressing.\n\nThe table has size M and initially all positions are empty.\nEach insertion gives a key and its home address h(x). If that position is occupied, resolve the collision by linear probing:\n(h(x) + i) mod M for i = 0, 1, 2, ...\nInsert the key into the first empty position found. If the table is full, ignore that insertion.\n\nAfter all insertions, process search queries. Each query gives a key and its home address h(x). Search the table using the same linear probing order. If the key is found, print its index. If an empty position is reached before finding the key, print -1. Also print -1 if the key is not found after probing all M positions.\n\nInput format:\n- First line: M N Q\n- Next N lines: key homeAddress\n- Next Q lines: key homeAddress\n\nConstraints for this task:\n- 0 <= homeAddress < M\n- Use -1 internally to represent an empty slot.\n\nOutput format:\n- Print Q lines, one result for each search query.",
    "source_chunk_ids": ["6a81d7efd103f7044396559e"],
    "skeleton_code": [
      {
        "filename": "Main.java",
        "content": "import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n\n        int M = sc.nextInt();\n        int N = sc.nextInt();\n        int Q = sc.nextInt();\n\n        int[] table = new int[M];\n        Arrays.fill(table, -1);\n\n        // Insert N keys using open addressing with linear probing.\n        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {\n            int key = sc.nextInt();\n            int home = sc.nextInt();\n\n        }\n\n        // Answer Q search queries.\n        for (int i = 0; i < Q; i++) {\n            int key = sc.nextInt();\n            int home = sc.nextInt();\n\n        }\n\n        sc.close();\n    }\n}\n",
        "is_readonly": false
