    "title": "Simulate a Circular Array Queue and Report Front/Last Indices",
    "description": "Write a Java program to simulate a queue stored in an array of size N using circular indexing.\n\nThe queue must follow First In First Out order. Use queue operations based on these rules:\n- ENQ x: insert x at the rear of the queue. If the queue is full, ignore this command.\n- DEQ: remove the front element. If the queue is empty, ignore this command.\n\nInput format:\n- The first line contains two integers N and M, where N is the array capacity and M is the number of commands.\n- The next M lines each contain one command: either ENQ x or DEQ.\n\nOutput format:\n- If the queue is empty after all commands, print:\n  - first line: -1 -1\n  - second line: EMPTY\n- Otherwise, print:\n  - first line: the index of the front element and the index of the last element\n  - second line: all elements currently in the queue from front to rear, separated by one space\n\nYour implementation must treat the array as circular, so indices can wrap from the end of the array back to 0.",
    "source_chunk_ids": ["6a826bc7801f2c79820f8ed1", "6a826bc7801f2c79820f8ecf"],
    "skeleton_code": [
      {
